#N canvas 20 20 4020 3960 12;
#X text 20 20 CUT IT DEBUG -- the five questions that otherwise need a laptop and a network \, answered from the front panel. What MIDI is arriving and on what channel \; whether each output device answers when you fire something at it \; what the error log says \; what address this Organelle is on \; and a re-wire by hand. ⛔ IT IS A SEPARATE PATCH AND THAT IS THE WHOLE REASON IT WORKS: the instrument's own Pd is what has usually stopped answering by the time you want any of this. See ref/module/debug.md., f 96;
#X text 20 200 ⚠️ SELECTING THIS STOPS THE INSTRUMENT. Loading any patch restarts Pd \, so this is for "Cut It is broken and I am not playing right now" and never for mid-set. ⛔ What u_state wrote is SAFE -- it lives in /sdcard/cut-it-state/ \, outside both patch folders \, precisely so a reload cannot touch it. ⚠️ The Launchpad is left in Programmer Mode if the instrument was killed rather than exited cleanly \, and its own Settings menu is locked out in that state \; tools/lp-live.sh restores it and needs no Pd., f 96;
#X text 20 380 ⛔ IT WIRES ITSELF \, AND THAT IS NOT OPTIONAL. Loading any patch DROPS Pd's ALSA connections \, so a debug patch that did not run wire.sh would measure silence and report it as "no MIDI arriving" -- the worst possible lie for this particular tool. wire.sh is idempotent \, costs about 247 ms \, and connects BY NAME because ALSA renumbers clients across a replug (items 287 and 292). ⛔ IT MUST NOT ASK THE presence BUS. That bus gained a re-wire selector for the phone \, but it lives inside Cut It's Pd instance and loading this patch is what killed that instance., f 96;
#X text 20 580 THE KEYBOARD IS THE MENU \, counting from the lowest key. k1 LEAVES -- it sends goHome and you are back in the Organelle's menu. k2 to k7 select the six screens. k13 \, k15 and k17 -- the C \, D and E an octave up -- fire the Volca probe \, the SP-404 probe and a re-wire \, and they work from ANY screen. ⚠️ A release publishes as velocity 0 \, so every key is gated on velocity above zero or every press would fire twice., f 96;
#X text 20 740 ⛔ THE ENCODER IS DELIBERATELY NOT TAKEN \, even though this patch is allowed to take it. Banging enableSubMenu makes mother forward enc and encbut -- but it is an OVERRIDE and it takes the CLICK as well \, which is the press that leaves a running patch. A debug tool you cannot get out of at a venue is worse than one you steer with the keys. Items 313 and 314 \, ref/device/organelle.md. If it is ever taken \, k1 above is already the way out and the screen already says so., f 96;
#X text 20 920 ⛔ EVERY LINE DRAWN IS A SINGLE SYMBOL \, WITH DASHES WHERE YOU WANT SPACES. mother's screenLine chain is r screenLineN -> list -> list append send /oled/line/N -> oscOut \, so a multi-atom message does arrive -- but whether the display joins the arguments with spaces has never been measured on this device \, and this is the one tool that must not surprise you. Dashes are ugly and certain. makefilename builds every dynamic one., f 96;
#X text 20 1100 ⛔ err-tail.sh AND net-probe.sh EACH EMIT A FIXED NUMBER OF LINES \, four and three \, and the patch routes them to screen rows BY POSITION. A script that emitted a variable count would move the newest error to a different row every time. Both say so in their own headers. ⚠️ THE TWO DATA SCRIPTS SHARE ONE shell AND wire.sh HAS ITS OWN \, so a slow re-wire cannot interleave its output into the line router., f 96;
#X text 20 1280 THERE IS NO CONSOLE AND THAT IS WHY THE SCREEN IS THE OUTPUT. Pd runs -nogui here and stdout goes to tty1 \, which VNC will not show -- the same reason every stage patch under tools/ writes to /sdcard/ and instructs on the OLED. ⚠️ PROVE THE PROBE BEFORE BELIEVING THE SILENCE: if the MIDI screen shows nothing \, move a nanoKONTROL fader -- a device you KNOW is transmitting -- before concluding anything is dead. Reporting silence is this patch's whole job \, which is exactly what makes a false silence expensive., f 96;
#X obj 20 1560 loadbang;
#X obj 20 1610 del 1500;
#X obj 20 1660 t b b b;
#X text 240 1560 ⚠️ loadbang FIRES BEFORE THE ALSA CONNECTIONS EXIST. Anything sent on loadbang itself goes nowhere at all \, which is why wire.sh is forked after a delay rather than with the bang., f 44;
#X obj 20 1730 t b b;
#X text 240 1730 THE RE-WIRE TRIGGER \, shared by the boot chain and by k17 so both count and both fork. Right outlet first: the run is counted \, then the fork happens., f 44;
#X msg 20 1810 sh wire.sh;
#X obj 20 1860 shell;
#X obj 20 1910 t a a;
#X obj 320 1960 print WIRE;
#X obj 20 1960 list append;
#X obj 20 2010 list split 1;
#X obj 20 2060 list split 1;
#X obj 20 2110 list trim;
#X obj 20 2160 makefilename links-%d;
#X obj 20 2210 symbol -;
#X text 240 2010 wire.sh's LAST LINE IS ITS OWN REPORT -- "wire.sh: N connections" -- and this pulls the N out of it: list append gives the message a list selector \, the first split drops the label \, the second takes the count off the front. ⛔ A SILENT ROT IF THAT LINE EVER CHANGES SHAPE: the store keeps its dash and screen 5 shows links-none forever rather than going red. The symbol object holds it so a repaint can ask again., f 52;
#X obj 700 1730 f;
#X obj 700 1780 + 1;
#X obj 700 1830 t f f;
#X obj 700 1890 f;
#X obj 700 1950 makefilename runs-%d;
#X text 900 1830 THE MIRROR IS WHAT LETS A REPAINT READ A COUNT WITHOUT CHANGING IT. Banging the counter's own f would push the value through + 1 again \, so every repaint would count itself. The right outlet stores \, the left outlet feeds a second f that only ever gets written -- and the repaint bangs that one. Every counter below has the same four boxes for the same reason., f 52;
#X obj 1500 1560 notein;
#X obj 1800 1560 ctlin;
#X obj 1800 1620 t f f;
#X obj 1800 1680 - 1;
#X obj 1800 1730 div 16;
#X obj 1500 1790 f;
#X obj 2150 1680 f;
#X obj 1500 1730 t b;
#X obj 1500 1850 select 0 1 2 3;
#X text 1500 1910 ⛔ THE PORT IS THE DEVICE \, because wire.sh says so: Launchpad on Pd channels 1-16 \, nanoKONTROL 17-32 \, SP-404 33-48 \, the USB Uno interface 49-64. So (channel-1)/16 names which box the event came out of \, with no device having to identify itself. ⚠️ BOTH OBJECTS ARE OMNI and both fire CHANNEL FIRST \, rightmost outlet -- so the port is stored before the pitch or the value arrives to read it. That ordering is the whole trick and it is the same one m_404 uses., f 62;
#X text 2150 1740 THE RAW CHANNEL IS KEPT TOO \, and it is the answer to the second half of the question: the port says which device \, the channel says which of its channels -- which for the SP-404 is the BANK \, 33 to 42. Written cold on every event \, read only on repaint., f 46;
#X obj 1500 2080 f;
#X obj 1500 2130 + 1;
#X obj 1500 2180 t f f;
#X obj 1500 2240 f;
#X obj 1500 2300 makefilename lp-%d;
#X obj 1850 2080 f;
#X obj 1850 2130 + 1;
#X obj 1850 2180 t f f;
#X obj 1850 2240 f;
#X obj 1850 2300 makefilename nano-%d;
#X obj 2200 2080 f;
#X obj 2200 2130 + 1;
#X obj 2200 2180 t f f;
#X obj 2200 2240 f;
#X obj 2200 2300 makefilename sp404-%d;
#X obj 2550 2240 makefilename ch-%d;
#X text 1500 2440 ⚠️ THE USB Uno INTERFACE GETS NO COUNTER OF ITS OWN and that is a choice \, not an oversight: the Volca behind it is RECEIVE-ONLY and has no MIDI out at all \, so port 4 has nothing to count unless something is plugged into the interface's DIN IN jack. Anything that ever is shows up in ch- as a channel between 49 and 64 \, which is enough to notice it. Four rows \, four facts \, and no row that is always zero., f 62;
#X msg 2900 1560 60 100 49;
#X obj 2900 1610 unpack f f f;
#X obj 3150 1560 del 200;
#X msg 3150 1610 60 0 49;
#X obj 3150 1660 unpack f f f;
#X msg 2900 1760 48 100 33;
#X obj 2900 1810 unpack f f f;
#X obj 3150 1760 del 200;
#X msg 3150 1810 48 0 33;
#X obj 3150 1860 unpack f f f;
#X obj 2900 1960 noteout;
#X text 2900 2020 ⛔ THE PROBES ARE THE ONES THE PHONE ALREADY SETTLED -- notes 60 100 200 at the Volca and pad 1 100 at the SP-404 \, item 306 -- and they are reused rather than invented a second time. noteout's CHANNEL ENCODES THE PORT \, so 49 reaches the Volca's slot and 33 reaches the 404's bank A with no port argument anywhere. unpack fires RIGHT TO LEFT \, which hands noteout the channel and the velocity before the pitch that triggers it -- the ordering m_volca and m_404 both depend on., f 62;
#X text 2900 2200 ⚠️ PAD A1 MAY HOLD A LOOPING SAMPLE AND THE PATCH CANNOT STOP IT. The note-off is scheduled correctly -- the loop is the 404's own sample setting -- so nothing here and no All Notes Off will end it. Press the pad on the device. Item 311 \, kept anyway because a probe's job is to make a noise you can identify and a loop is easy to identify., f 62;
#X obj 2900 2340 f;
#X obj 2900 2390 + 1;
#X obj 2900 2440 t f f;
#X obj 2900 2500 f;
#X obj 2900 2560 makefilename sent-%d;
#X msg 20 2400 sh err-tail.sh;
#X msg 320 2400 sh net-probe.sh;
#X obj 20 2460 shell;
#X obj 20 2510 t l b;
#X msg 620 2400 1;
#X obj 300 2560 f;
#X obj 300 2610 + 1;
#X obj 300 2660 t f f;
#X obj 20 2720 list prepend;
#X obj 20 2780 route 1 2 3 4;
#X obj 20 2840 list trim;
#X obj 320 2840 list trim;
#X obj 620 2840 list trim;
#X obj 920 2840 list trim;
#X text 300 2960 THE LINE ROUTER \, and it is why both scripts have a fixed line count. Each line out of shell bumps an index \, the index is prepended \, and route sends line 1 to screenLine2 and so on down. ⛔ THE INDEX IS RESET BEFORE EVERY FORK \, from the draw chain \, not after the last line -- there is no way to know which line is the last one. ⚠️ t l b fires the BANG FIRST \, so the index is set into list prepend's cold inlet before the line reaches the hot one., f 78;
#X obj 1500 2600 r notes;
#X obj 1500 2660 unpack f f;
#X obj 1750 2720 > 0;
#X obj 1500 2780 spigot;
#X obj 1500 2840 select 60 61 62 63 64 65 66 72 74 76;
#X text 2400 2740 ⚠️ THE VELOCITY GATE IS NOT COSMETIC. unpack fires the velocity first \, which sets the spigot \, and the pitch follows through it -- so a release at velocity 0 is dropped and one press fires once. Without it every key would act twice \, and k13 would send the Volca two notes for one finger., f 78;
#X obj 2100 2960 s goHome;
#X msg 1500 2960 1;
#X msg 1560 2960 2;
#X msg 1620 2960 3;
#X msg 1680 2960 4;
#X msg 1740 2960 5;
#X msg 1800 2960 6;
#X obj 1500 3010 t b f;
#X obj 1500 3050 f;
#X obj 1500 3090 select 1 2 3 4 5 6;
#X obj 1900 3010 metro 300;
#X text 2200 3010 THE SCREEN NUMBER LIVES IN ONE f AND EVERYTHING READS IT. A key stores it and then bangs it \, in that order -- t b f fires its RIGHT outlet first -- so the new screen is drawn immediately instead of waiting up to 300 ms for the metro. ⛔ THE METRO IS WHAT MAKES THE COUNTERS LIVE: nothing else repaints \, so without it screen 1 would freeze at whatever it said when you selected it \, which is precisely the wrong lie for a monitor., f 78;
#X obj 100 3300 t b b b b b;
#X msg 100 3360 symbol 1-MIDI-IN;
#X obj 500 3300 t b b b b b;
#X msg 500 3360 symbol 2-TEST-OUT;
#X msg 620 3410 symbol k13-volca-n60;
#X msg 740 3460 symbol k15-sp404-padA1;
#X msg 860 3510 symbol A1-may-LOOP;
#X obj 1200 3300 t b b b;
#X msg 1200 3360 symbol 3-ERR-LOG;
#X obj 1500 3300 t b b b b;
#X msg 1500 3360 symbol 4-NETWORK;
#X msg 1620 3410 symbol k17-re-wires;
#X obj 1900 3300 t b b b b b;
#X msg 1900 3360 symbol 5-RE-WIRE;
#X msg 2020 3410 symbol k17-runs-wire.sh;
#X msg 2140 3460 symbol by-name-not-number;
#X obj 2500 3300 t b b b b b;
#X msg 2500 3360 symbol 6-HELP;
#X msg 2620 3410 symbol k1-exits-to-menu;
#X msg 2740 3460 symbol k2-k7-pick-screen;
#X msg 2860 3510 symbol k13-volca-k15-404;
#X msg 2980 3560 symbol k17-re-wire;
#X text 100 3760 ONE DRAW CHAIN PER SCREEN \, and each one writes EVERY row it owns rather than only the ones that changed. A chain that wrote three rows would leave the fourth showing the previous screen's text \, which is the failure mode that makes a diagnostic tool lie. Screens 3 and 4 write their title and then fork \, so their rows arrive a moment later -- see the router above., f 96;
#X obj 2500 3620 s screenLine1;
#X obj 2620 3620 s screenLine2;
#X obj 2740 3620 s screenLine3;
#X obj 2860 3620 s screenLine4;
#X obj 2980 3620 s screenLine5;
#X obj 2900 1500 t b b b;
#X obj 3400 1700 t b b b;
#X text 2500 1400 ⛔ ONE TRIGGER PER PROBE \, RATHER THAN LETTING THE MESSAGE BOX FAN OUT. The note-on message \, the note-off delay and the sent counter are three destinations \, and C-3 wants the order stated even where any order would work. Rightmost first: count \, arm the off \, then play., f 46;
#X obj 1500 2360 s screenLine2;
#X obj 1850 2360 s screenLine3;
#X obj 2200 2360 s screenLine4;
#X obj 2550 2300 s screenLine5;
#X obj 20 2270 s screenLine3;
#X obj 700 2010 s screenLine4;
#X obj 2900 2620 s screenLine4;
#X text 700 2070 ⛔ A SECOND s screenLine4 IS NOT A SECOND OWNER. This patch owns all five rows \, and a send placed beside the thing that feeds it is how Pd keeps a cord short -- the alternative is a cord across the whole canvas to one shared box \, which is layout nobody can read. C-5's one-owner rule is about the SURFACE \, and here the surface has exactly one patch on it., f 46;
